# Manifest CLI Configuration
manifest.config
.manifest_last_update
.last_update_check

# Manifest CLI — per-repo machine-local state (release-gate pass ledger, etc).
# This is the same entry create_default_gitignore writes into every repo
# Manifest scaffolds (modules/core/manifest-init.sh). It was missing HERE, so
# the gate wrote .manifest-cli/release-gate-pass.epoch mid-ship, the bare
# `git add .` auto-commit swept it, and every release carried a junk commit
# containing the ship's own artifact — 31 of them before anyone noticed. The
# module comment on _manifest_gate_pass_ledger_file says this file is "never
# committed"; that is true again now. Guarded by tests/gitignore_self_apply.bats.
.manifest-cli/
*.manifest-cli.log

# Manifest CLI — TTL'd green-run test cache (run-tests.sh, never commit)
.test-cache/

# Audit runs — local evidence, never committed.
# The release commit is a bare `git add .`, so without this an audit left in the
# tree gets swept into the next release. Keeping them untracked is also what the
# audits themselves assume: each locks a baseline of the tracked tree (277 files
# at 93fe4ac), and committing its own output would invalidate that baseline.
# Deliberately a prefix glob so future audit runs are covered without an edit.
CODEX_AUDIT_*

# Manifest CLI — Local overrides (NEVER commit)
manifest.config.local.yaml

# Legacy Manifest CLI env files (NEVER commit)
.env.manifest.local

# Private Environment Files (NEVER commit these!)
#
# The literal list below was the whole rule until 2026-08-24, which meant
# `.env.dev` and `.env.ci` — shapes the Manifest .gitignore template ignores in
# every repo it scaffolds — were unignored HERE. Same self-application gap as
# the .manifest-cli/ one above: the tool wrote the rule into other people's
# repos and not its own. The wildcard now leads and the literals are kept only
# because they document intent. Escape hatch: `git add -f <path>`.
.env
.env.*
.env.local
.env.development
.env.test
.env.production
.env.staging
.env.*.local
.env.*.backup
.env.*.tmp
*.env
*.env.local

# Re-included: these are templates, not secrets, and MUST stay after `.env.*`.
!.env.example
!.env.template

# Local overrides and secret-shaped files of any format.
# `manifest.config.local.yaml` is listed separately above; these cover the
# shapes this CLI itself defines but never ignored here — notably
# `manifest.fleet.config.local.yaml` (see modules/core/manifest-config.sh).
*.local.yaml
*.local.yml
*.local.json
*.local.toml
*.secret.yaml
*.secret.yml
*.secret.json
*.secret.*

# Editor/tool temp and merge artifacts.
# These are in the template but were absent here, and since the ship-time
# cleanup sweep was correctly narrowed to the docs tree, nothing deletes them
# tree-wide either — so a stray one was both unignored AND unswept, and the
# release commit's bare `git add .` would take it. Neither half covered it.
*.tmp
*.temp
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
tmp/
temp/

# User-specific configuration
.manifestrc
.manifestrc.local
.manifestrc.*.local

# Secrets and credentials
secrets/
secrets.yml
secrets.yaml
secrets.json

# Certificates (public halves — kept ignored here by local convention)
*.crt
*.cer

# ============================================================================
#  KEY MATERIAL
# ============================================================================
# Deny-by-default. Manifest's release commit is a bare `git add .` and its
# security gate does not scan for key material, so an unmatched private key
# gets pushed to the remote permanently, on the next ship, silently.
#
# Escape hatch for a legitimately-public file caught below:
#     git add -f path/to/public-thing.pem

# --- PEM-family containers: the extension does not tell you what's inside ---
*.key
*.pem
*.p8
*.pkcs8
*.der

# --- Binary bundles: these always carry the private half ---
*.pfx
*.p12
*.jks
*.keystore
*.bks

# --- SSH private keys ---
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
id_ecdsa_sk
id_ed25519_sk
*_rsa
*_ed25519
*.ppk

# --- GPG private keyrings only. NOT *.gpg — that is ciphertext and is often
#     committed deliberately (git-crypt, sops, pass). ---
secring.*
*.private.gpg
*.secret.gpg

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  EXCEPTIONS — public halves, re-included. Must stay AFTER the rules above.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!*.pub
!*public*.pem
!*public*.key
!fullchain.pem
!chain.pem

# Cloud provider credentials
.aws/
.azure/
.gcloud/
gcloud-credentials.json
aws-credentials.json
azure-credentials.json

# API keys and tokens
.api-keys
api-keys.json
*.token
token.txt

# OS-generated files
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
ehthumbs.db
Thumbs.db

# Editor files
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# AI assistants and coding agents
# Personal agent state stays local; the team-shared subset is re-included.
# Ignored by CONTENT (dir/*) — git cannot re-include inside an excluded directory.
.aider*
.claude/*
!.claude/agents/
!.claude/commands/
# .claude/settings.json is deliberately NOT re-included (untracked 2026-08-16).
# Claude Code writes "don't ask again" permission grants into it as you work, and
# those grants embed absolute paths (/Users/<you>/…). Because Manifest's release
# commit is a bare `git add .`, a tracked settings.json means every ship tries to
# commit whatever the IDE last wrote there. That is what blocked the v59.1.0 ship.
# It was sanitized by hand once (63a8ed5) and re-polluted by three auto-commits;
# 16 of its 19 rules were one-off command strings, so it never functioned as
# curated team config. Agent/command definitions above ARE still shared — those
# are authored, not machine-generated. See TRACKER §7.
.codeium/
.continue/
.cursor/*
!.cursor/rules/
.gemini/
.windsurf/*
!.windsurf/rules/
!.windsurf/workflows/

